0..30 Comment "//! `map` obeys functoriality."
31..34 Comment "//!"
35..83 Comment "//! A composite map equals two consecutive maps."
84..133 Comment "//! The list source gives `map` a function value."
134..137 Comment "//!"
138..188 Comment "//! A rule names only the operations of its block."
189..260 Comment "//! A defunctionalised `map` exposes `app` and `compose` as operations."
261..315 Comment "//! The coincidence check uses the definition\'s rules."
316..359 Comment "//! The `normalizes` row exercises the law."
360..363 Comment "//!"
364..427 Comment "//! - cites: [Bird-1987-an_introduction_to_the_theory_of_lists]"
428..516 Comment "//! - cites: [Reynolds-1972-definitional_interpreters_higherorder_programming_languages]"
517..598 Comment "//! - see: `../../pathological/data/map-law-on-a-def.gandr` — the same law over"
599..625 Comment "//!   the `def` is refused"
626..629 Comment "//!"
630..776 Comment "//! [Bird-1987-an_introduction_to_the_theory_of_lists]: https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-87374-4_1 \"Bird, An Introduction to the Theory of Lists\""
777..972 Comment "//! [Reynolds-1972-definitional_interpreters_higherorder_programming_languages]: https://doi.org/10.1145/800194.805852 \"Reynolds, Definitional Interpreters for Higher-Order Programming Languages\""
974..1019 Comment "/// `ListMap` defines `map` and its branches."
1020..1066 Comment "/// It defines composition and fuses two maps."
